n for  what they want this campaign to be but  now we're back to boots on the ground  make it feel like it's in the war yeah  not just outside the war or a part of  the war it is in the middle of it  strategic and fast-paced and grounded  but rooted in a cause that's really  remarkable everything has to be detailed  down to like you know the buttons on the  pockets and patches on the right side to  not only bring this to life and a fun  way for fans to make sure that the  storytelling  honors that conflict I have no problem  with this if you're going to set a game  during a real extremely bloody conflict  you're straddling a tough line between  being honest and making a fun game to  begin with  that's a tough challenge but  sledgehammer doesn't do much with its  authenticity its characters are all  stereotypes back home you want to settle  something you do it head-on it's the  only way to earn respect the main plot  revolves around a hard-headed sergeant  and a compassionate lieutenant who often  butt heads  it's a slew of cliches that explore the  tension between the mission and empathy  and once again it regurgitates scenes  we've seen in countless other games  welcome to the bloody first you're a  long way from Texas farm boy even last  year's infinite warfare asked more  compelling questions about the toll of  leadership and right when you think  World War two story might delve into  tough but compelling subjects such as  the experience of Nazi labor camps or  your relationship with a person of color  during the Battle of the Bulge it veers  away these moments seem to exist just so  sledgehammer can say look isn't this  true to the time period before moving on  to the next set piece  [Music]  Call of Duty World War two's campaign is  all the more disappointing when you  remember that the franchise rates  created specifically as a reaction to  Medal of Honor which became redundant  with each new entry and strayed from its  so-called authentic routes infinity  ward's Vince Zampella and Jason West at  the time wanted to return to what made  their earlier games great and with the  first Call of Duty in 2003 they  succeeded since then the series has  gradually slipped away from its  historical roots it let you battle  robots super soldiers and fictional  spies by doing so a series shrugged off  the weight of a dark historical period  in going back to the place where it all  started the team was assuming a huge  risk  but would call duty World War two  sledgehammer doesn't really earn its  setting during the Second Great War it's  a mishmash of movies and games we've  seen numerous times already and when it  does show potential it usually squanders  it no this game does not look like it  could have released in 2003 but it sure  feels like it  it's as if sledgehammer wanted to simply  make the best looking World War 2  experience and not the most compelling  if they were trying to make the next  great action blockbuster they fell we've  seen this all before  [Music]
 

